From 0a9f3b34541bfa7f63110354dc30cfd64beaf49c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djsaunde <1245942+djsaunde@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:12:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 22/22] fast: asynchronous colour and depth read-backs,
 read-back and shader counters

Off by default (Interpreter::mReadbackAsync). With it, Run() only queues the read-backs: the colour requests'
scaled copy (as before, GPU-side) and, for depth, a grid of every native pixel, made GPU-side: the depth is
blitted 1:1 into a texture, and a draw fetches each grid pixel's source (the pixel GetPixelDepth reads, its
coordinates converted the same way) and writes it as the fragment depth into a native-size depth buffer. A fence
follows. FinishReadbacks() waits for the fence and reads the small copies back (300 KB), then answers the colour
requests and ReadbackGetDepth as before; ReadbackGridDepth answers any native pixel of that frame, and
ReadbackDepthGridNow reads the grid of the frame drawn last at once. A port that takes the answers at the game's
next step, as 1080's native host does, gets the same values at the same step, and the CPU no longer waits for the
GPU at the frame's end: a glReadPixels while the frame is still being drawn waits for all of it, and on GL over
D3D12 (Mesa d3d12, WSLg) or Metal it is a full flush each time; even a read into a pixel-pack buffer waits on
d3d12. The depth grid replaces one blit per requested pixel (169 per sun pixel with the port's window) and one
synchronous read per depth pixel the game reads at once.

The rendering API gets ReadFramebufferToCPUBegin / Finish and ReadDepthGridBegin / Finish, synchronous by
default; the OpenGL backend implements them (GL 3.0 / ES 3.0: fences, an integer texture, texelFetch). OpenGL
ES writes the grid's depth as 24 bits in RGBA8, as its GetPixelDepth does.

Interpreter::mReadbackStats (read-backs that waited, and the time) and mShaderStats (shader programs built, and
the time) feed a port's perf log.
